suit up and hold onto your heads because  we have the second scariest Frank on  Netflix next to this guy there is but  one rule hunt or be hunted with less  than a week until the release of the new  season of The Punisher I thought it'd be  fun to see where we left off in season 1  and explain what really happened in that  bloody finale season 1 ended with an  absolutely brutal fight between Frank  Castle aka The Punisher and best friend  turned backstabbing [ __ ]  Billy Russo nice that's a knife Frank  ultimately gains the upper hand and  rather than kill Billy shoves his face  into shards of glass so that he can live  with the reminder of the pain he's  caused in the trailer for season 2 it  looks like he'll definitely be back but  that choice to let someone evil like  Billy live is a new one for Frank and  it's important in understanding Frank as  a character and where you might be  heading into season 2 the entire season  Frank goes on and on about how he'll  kill anyone who gets in his way  but with Billy he doesn't go through  with it the most evil of them all and he  doesn't do it to understand this  decision we have to go all the way back  to episode 1 in episode 1 Frank is in a  dark place reeling from the death of his  family using a secret alias living in a  shitty apartment and constantly harassed  by his fellow construction co-workers  who call him a [ __ ]  Frank is lost unsure what life has in  store for him rattled by grief with no  will to live he even keeps his head down  trying not to get involved with  potential new friends like Donnie  because everyone he gets close to seems  to die and what better metaphor to show  the barriers Frank needs to overcome  than a concrete wall the wall is his  guilt the belief that he was responsible  for the death of his wife and children  we see him violently chip away at it to  the point where his hands become a  bloody mess his last name Castle alludes  to the impenetrable walls of medieval  castles but instead of keeping out  foreign invaders for Frank it's the pain  and emotions guilt is one of the biggest  themes this season almost every  character has some form of it Frank for  the death of his family and also how he  treated his son in a flashback he  laments how he scolded his son on a  ferry ride and when his son was younger  chastised him for drawing it  of a marine on the wall not realizing he  drew it because he wanted to be just  like his dad he also feels responsible  for Kurtis losing his leg he couldn't  shoot the pregnant woman who had a bomb  in Afghanistan which led to the loss of  it the name The Punisher not only  relates to how Frank treats his victims  but how he treats himself he is punished  every day by the memory of his dead  family it haunts him other characters  such as David Lieberman aka Mike Rowe  who ends up joining Frank and his  crusade feels the guilt of not being  there for his family the torture of  watching them without being with them is  a common thread throughout the season  David's wife Sarah drinks copious  amounts of wine during the day to cover  the guilt she has over not being able to  manage her children you're not gonna  judge me for appreciating little Rosie  her son she even finds carrying a knife  around in his backpack  a plot point which ends up leading to  one of the most interesting and  emotional scenes in the season  officer Deena Madani has guilt over the  death of her partner Sam Stein who was  killed by Billy Russo on a fake decoy op  that was entirely her plan but the  person who doesn't show any signs of  guilt at all you guessed it Billy Russo  in fact he's pretty remorseless the  writers even added scenes that  technically do not add to the plot at  all just to flesh out how evil this guy  is for example in episode 8 we find out  that Billy has his heroin addict mother  tied to the hospital bed to live in  torment for the rest of her life he's a  keeper Madani Frank is equally ruthless  but what differentiates him from Billy  is that he has a code he kills only the  bad and isn't interested in power or  money like Billy's take how the two of  them approach situations in which they  need to escape in episode 7 when Frank  flees from the military base he's  stopped by a young soldier instead of  just taking him out Frank warns him  he doesn't want to hurt him contrast  that to Billy who completely goes on a  rampage when leaving anvil to fight  Frank but everything comes to a head  when Frank and Billy faceoff at the  Carousel the same location Frank's  family was brutally murdered and Billy  knows this this trauma PTSD if you will  will give billion upper-hand since Frank  must not only fight Billy but his own  Demon's if he's going to win the day and  the carousel is personified by Frank  himself both go around in circles it  never goes anywhere just round and round  it's usually a symbol of youth and  innocence there are even two young  employees who are tied up as hostages  but here the carousel is one of blood  and violence after the vicious battle  between Frank and Billy Frank lets Billy  lives stating Gigi this whole season has  culminated in this decision  Frank has learned that the pain of  living with lofts far outweighs the easy  way out of killing yourself the pain of  waking up and looking for your loved  ones only to be reminded that they are  dead is a hell in and of itself so he  decides to completely tear apart Billy's  face as a reminder of him in fact this  notion of him being a reminder is  brought up in episode 12 after being  brought to the brink of death Frank  experiences visions of himself and his  wife she encourages him to come with her  which would mean his death but Frank  decides to push through dying is the  coward's way out he must fight and after  he kills Rawlins he proclaims that he  will live on as a reminder on the other  hand we have Billy who pleads with Frank  to kill him he'd rather die than live  and be reminded of what Frank did to him  but Frank knows it would be worse for  Billy to let him live that is his  punishment as he says in episode three  people tainted or Jewish Payne's not be  it's done it's time slowly realize that  your life itself he wants Billy to live  in this torture what this show does well  is slowly create change in Frank it  doesn't happen like a flip of a switch  he has to see for himself the error of  his worldview before he can truly change  my favourite example of this is when  Frank teaches David's son a lesson by  holding a knife to his neck  ah good father/son talk we learned the  reason the kid had the knife in the  first place was that he was lonely and  sad and that he wanted to make others  feel bad like he does what better mirror  to hold up to Frank who essentially does  the same thing and mirrors are often  symbols of confronting our inner selves  like the mirrors both Billy and Frank  look into at the carousels mirrors which  Frank avoids in episode 1 but must  confront in episode 13 he's really look  in the mirror you get it yeah you gotta  admit who you are but not just to  yourself you could be and everybody else  so while the Punisher may be a  shoot-'em-up action extravaganza it's  also the story of a man struggling with  grief trying to overcome loss and move  on because if you don't you'll just end  up on that carousel round and round  until it's too late thanks everyone for  watching I got a whole bunch more stuff  than we have here the end of January and  I'm hoping to do an analysis of season  two so make sure you're subscribed with  the Belon to get my latest videos  we're almost at 100k so a huge thank you  to everyone  and remember daddy loves you very much
 

